LOCAL84="Packages built for a newer processor"
LOCAL85="The installed packages %s are built for a higher x86-64 level than this processor offers (it offers %s), so a program from them may stop with an illegal instruction. This happens when a disk is moved to an older computer. Repair installs them again and apt takes the baseline build for this processor (sudo sparky-stereo-hardware --variants). The notice ends when they are baseline again."
LOCAL86="Lights and peripherals: one program for each device"
LOCAL87="OpenLinkHub and OpenRGB both open %s, and read together they cross each other's replies (a power supply was shown at about 1,800 W). OpenLinkHub is installed, so it owns this device and OpenRGB's access to it is switched off. To give OpenRGB the device back, put an empty file named 62-sparky-stereo-peripheral-owner.rules in /etc/udev/rules.d, then unplug and plug the device again."
LOCAL88="OpenLinkHub from its own installer"
LOCAL89="OpenLinkHub from upstream's own installer (/opt/OpenLinkHub) is on this computer, and the package openlinkhub is installed beside it. The package left your copy exactly as it is: its service is not enabled, and nothing was moved or changed. To change over to the package, run: sudo systemctl disable --now OpenLinkHub; sudo mkdir -p /var/lib/openlinkhub; sudo cp -a /opt/OpenLinkHub/database /opt/OpenLinkHub/config.json /var/lib/openlinkhub/; sudo chown -R openlinkhub:openlinkhub /var/lib/openlinkhub; sudo rm /etc/systemd/system/OpenLinkHub.service /etc/udev/rules.d/99-openlinkhub.rules; sudo systemctl daemon-reload; sudo udevadm control --reload-rules; sudo systemctl enable --now OpenLinkHub. The same steps are in /usr/share/doc/openlinkhub/README.Debian. Nothing is moved for you."
LOCAL90="LACT from its own makefile"
LOCAL91="A copy of LACT made by upstream's makefile (/usr/local) is on this computer, and the package lact is installed beside it. The package left your copy exactly as it is: its service is not enabled, and nothing was moved or changed. To change over to the package, run: sudo systemctl disable --now lactd; sudo rm /usr/local/bin/lact /usr/local/lib/systemd/system/lactd.service; sudo systemctl daemon-reload; sudo systemctl enable --now lactd. Your settings in /etc/lact/config.yaml stay and the package's service reads them. The same steps are in /usr/share/doc/lact/README.Debian. Nothing is moved for you."
LOCAL92="HeadsetControl from its own makefile"
LOCAL93="A copy of HeadsetControl made by upstream's makefile (/usr/local) is on this computer, and the package headsetcontrol is installed beside it. The package left your copy exactly as it is, and nothing was moved or changed. Your copy and its rules in /usr/local take precedence over the package's. To change over to the package, run: sudo rm /usr/local/bin/headsetcontrol /usr/local/lib/udev/rules.d/70-headsets.rules; sudo udevadm control --reload-rules. Nothing is moved for you."
